WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.882
This query creates a diagnosis summary materialized view.

00:00:03.882 --> 00:00:06.947
The result has one row per diagnosis.

00:00:06.947 --> 00:00:11.542
It stores admission count, distinct patient count, average stay, and a refresh timestamp.

00:00:11.542 --> 00:00:17.692
The refresh timestamp documents when the snapshot was generated.

00:00:17.692 --> 00:00:21.452
It does not update every time someone reads the materialized view.

00:00:21.452 --> 00:00:26.057
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:26.057 --> 00:00:32.217
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:32.217 --> 00:00:34.737
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:40.862
In a real setting such as a governance project limiting which columns different users can access,

00:00:40.862 --> 00:00:46.807
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:46.807 --> 00:00:49.012
before discussing syntax.

00:00:49.012 --> 00:00:55.239
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
